1
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
People should not be afraid of their governments.
Governments should be afraid of their people.

2
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,000
V for Vendetta is a action, thriller, science
fiction film directed by James McTeigue.

3
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:06,000
In a world in which Great Britain has become a fascist state, a masked vigilante known only
as “V” conducts guerrilla warfare against the oppressive British government.

4
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
When V rescues a young woman from the secret police, he finds in her an ally
with whom he can continue his fight to free the people of Britain.

5
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:06,000
Evey, played by Natalie Portman.

6
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:06,000
V / William Rookwood, played by Hugo Weaving.

7
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Finch, played by Stephen Rea.

8
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
Deitrich, played by Stephen Fry.

9
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:06,000
Adam Sutler, played by John Hurt.

10
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
Creedy, played by Tim Pigott-Smith.

11
01:06:00,000 --> 01:06:06,000
V for Vendetta is a 2005 dystopian thriller film directed by James McTeigue in
his directorial debut, from a screenplay by the Wachowskis.

12
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
It is based on the 1988–89 DC Vertigo Comics limited series graphic novel of
the same name by Alan Moore, David Lloyd, and Tony Weare.

13
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
The film, set in a future where a fascist totalitarian regime has subjugated the UK, centres on V (portrayed by Hugo Weaving), an anarchist and masked freedom fighter who
attempts to ignite a revolution through elaborate terrorist acts, and Evey Hammond (portrayed by Natalie Portman), a young woman caught up in V's mission.

14
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
Stephen Rea portrays a detective leading a desperate quest to stop V.

15
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Produced by Silver Pictures, Virtual Studios and Anarchos Productions, Inc., V
for Vendetta was originally scheduled for release by Warner Bros.

16
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:06,000
Pictures on 4 November 2005 (a day before the 400th Guy Fawkes Night), but was delayed; it instead opened in the United States on 17 March 2006, to mostly
positive reviews from critics and became a box office success, grossing $134.7 million against a production budget between $50–54 million.

17
01:42:00,000 --> 01:42:06,000
Alan Moore, dissatisfied with the film adaptations of his other works, From Hell (2001) and The League of
Extraordinary Gentlemen (2003), declined to watch the film and asked to not be credited or paid royalties.

18
01:48:00,000 --> 01:48:06,000
Some political groups have seen V for Vendetta as an allegory of oppression by
government; anarchists have used it to promote their beliefs.

19
01:54:00,000 --> 01:54:06,000
The film is credited for popularizing the use of the Guy Fawkes mask
by anti-establishment political groups and activities.

20
02:00:00,000 --> 02:00:06,000
The film explores themes of government,
detective, revolution, dystopia, fascism.

21
02:06:00,000 --> 02:06:06,000
The tone is thought_provoking and intense.
